# Project Constitution: Crypto Realtime

## 1. Code Quality Principles

### 1.1 TypeScript Standards
- **Strict typing required**: All code must use TypeScript with strict mode enabled
- **No implicit any**: Explicitly type all variables, parameters, and return types
- **Type safety over convenience**: Prefer type-safe patterns over dynamic typing workarounds
- **Interface over type aliases**: Use interfaces for object shapes to enable declaration merging

### 1.2 Code Organization
- **Single Responsibility**: Each component, function, and module should have one clear purpose
- **DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself)**: Extract reusable logic into shared utilities or hooks
- **File structure**: Maximum 250 lines per file; split larger files into logical modules
- **Named exports preferred**: Use named exports for better refactoring and tree-shaking

### 1.3 React Best Practices
- **Functional components only**: No class components unless absolutely necessary
- **React Compiler ready**: Write code compatible with React Compiler optimization
- **Hooks rules**: Follow all Rules of Hooks; use ESLint to enforce
- **Immutable state updates**: Never mutate state directly; use proper state update patterns
- **Memoization**: Use React.memo, useMemo, and useCallback appropriately for performance

### 1.4 ESLint Compliance
- **Zero warnings policy**: All ESLint warnings must be addressed before commit
- **No disabled rules**: Never disable ESLint rules inline unless documented with justification
- **Pre-commit linting**: Code must pass `npm run lint` before being committed

## 2. Testing Standards

### 2.1 Test Coverage Requirements
- **Minimum 80% coverage**: All new code must maintain or increase overall coverage
- **Critical paths 100%**: Core business logic and data flows require complete coverage
- **Component testing**: Every UI component must have unit tests
- **Integration tests**: API integrations and WebSocket connections require integration tests

### 2.2 Test Quality Standards
- **Arrange-Act-Assert pattern**: Structure all tests clearly with setup, execution, and verification
- **Test isolation**: Each test must be independent and not rely on execution order
- **Descriptive test names**: Use clear, behavior-focused descriptions (e.g., "should display error when WebSocket fails")
- **Mock external dependencies**: All external APIs, WebSockets, and third-party services must be mocked

### 2.3 Testing Requirements by Type
- **Unit tests**: All utility functions, custom hooks, and business logic
- **Component tests**: All React components with user interactions and edge cases
- **Integration tests**: WebSocket connections, API endpoints, and data flow
- **E2E tests**: Critical user journeys (viewing crypto prices, real-time updates)

### 2.4 Test Execution
- **Fast tests**: Unit tests should complete in <5 seconds total
- **CI/CD integration**: All tests must pass before deployment
- **Watch mode**: Use test watch mode during development for immediate feedback

## 3. User Experience Consistency

### 3.1 Design System Adherence
- **Component library**: Use consistent, reusable components from the design system
- **No one-off styles**: All styling must follow established patterns and variables
- **Spacing system**: Use predefined spacing units (4px, 8px, 16px, 24px, 32px, 48px)
- **Color palette**: Strictly use defined color tokens; no arbitrary colors

### 3.2 Responsive Design
- **Mobile-first approach**: Design and develop for mobile, then enhance for desktop
- **Breakpoint consistency**: Use standard breakpoints (mobile: <768px, tablet: 768-1024px, desktop: >1024px)
- **Touch targets**: Minimum 44x44px touch targets for interactive elements
- **Fluid typography**: Use responsive font sizing with rem units

### 3.3 Accessibility (WCAG 2.1 AA)
- **Semantic HTML**: Use proper HTML5 elements (nav, main, article, section)
- **ARIA attributes**: Add appropriate ARIA labels where semantic HTML is insufficient
- **Keyboard navigation**: All interactive elements must be keyboard accessible
- **Color contrast**: Minimum 4.5:1 for normal text, 3:1 for large text
- **Screen reader testing**: Test with at least one screen reader before release

### 3.4 Loading & Error States
- **Loading indicators**: Show loading state for any action taking >300ms
- **Skeleton screens**: Use skeleton loaders for content-heavy sections
- **Error boundaries**: Implement error boundaries to gracefully handle component failures
- **User feedback**: Provide clear, actionable error messages with recovery options

### 3.5 Real-Time Data Display
- **Visual indicators**: Clearly show when data is live vs. stale
- **Connection status**: Display WebSocket connection state to users
- **Graceful degradation**: Fall back to polling if WebSocket unavailable
- **Price change animations**: Use subtle animations to highlight price updates

## 4. Performance Requirements

### 4.1 Load Time Targets
- **First Contentful Paint (FCP)**: <1.5 seconds
- **Largest Contentful Paint (LCP)**: <2.5 seconds
- **Time to Interactive (TTI)**: <3.5 seconds
- **Total bundle size**: <250KB (gzipped) for initial load

### 4.2 Runtime Performance
- **60 FPS interactions**: All animations and interactions must maintain 60fps
- **WebSocket efficiency**: Batch updates to avoid excessive re-renders
- **Memory management**: No memory leaks; monitor with DevTools
- **CPU usage**: Keep main thread work <50ms per frame

### 4.3 Code Splitting & Lazy Loading
- **Route-based splitting**: Each route should be a separate chunk
- **Component lazy loading**: Large components loaded on demand
- **Third-party code**: Isolate vendor code into separate chunks
- **Dynamic imports**: Use dynamic imports for features not needed immediately

### 4.4 Asset Optimization
- **Image optimization**: Use WebP format with fallbacks; lazy load below-the-fold images
- **Font loading**: Use font-display: swap; limit custom fonts to 2 families
- **Tree shaking**: Ensure dead code elimination is working effectively
- **Compression**: Enable Brotli/Gzip compression for all text assets

### 4.5 Data & Network Efficiency
- **WebSocket throttling**: Limit update frequency to necessary intervals (e.g., 100ms)
- **Data normalization**: Avoid duplicate data in state
- **Selective updates**: Only re-render components affected by data changes
- **Request batching**: Batch multiple API calls when possible
- **Caching strategy**: Implement appropriate cache headers and service worker caching

### 4.6 Monitoring & Metrics
- **Performance budgets**: Enforce budgets in CI/CD pipeline
- **Real User Monitoring (RUM)**: Track actual user performance metrics
- **Core Web Vitals**: Monitor and maintain "Good" ratings for all metrics
- **Error tracking**: Monitor and alert on performance regressions

## 5. Development Workflow

### 5.1 Git Practices
- **Conventional commits**: Use conventional commit format (feat:, fix:, docs:, etc.)
- **Branch naming**: feature/*, bugfix/*, hotfix/* patterns
- **Pull request reviews**: All code requires at least one review approval
- **No direct commits**: Never commit directly to main branch

### 5.2 Code Review Standards
- **Review checklist**: Verify code quality, tests, performance, and accessibility
- **Constructive feedback**: Provide actionable suggestions with examples
- **Timely reviews**: Complete reviews within 24 hours
- **Self-review first**: Author must review their own PR before requesting review

### 5.3 Documentation
- **README updates**: Keep README current with setup and usage instructions
- **Code comments**: Comment "why" not "what"; complex logic requires explanation
- **API documentation**: Document all public APIs and component props
- **Changelog**: Maintain CHANGELOG.md following Keep a Changelog format

## 6. Security Principles

### 6.1 Data Security
- **No sensitive data in client**: Never store API keys or secrets in frontend code
- **Environment variables**: Use environment variables for configuration
- **XSS prevention**: Sanitize all user input and dynamic content
- **HTTPS only**: All connections must use secure protocols

### 6.2 Dependency Management
- **Regular updates**: Update dependencies monthly for security patches
- **Audit checks**: Run `npm audit` before each release
- **License compliance**: Verify all dependencies have compatible licenses
- **Minimal dependencies**: Evaluate necessity before adding new packages

## 7. Deployment & Release

### 7.1 Pre-Deployment Checklist
- ✓ All tests passing
- ✓ Lint checks clean
- ✓ Performance budgets met
- ✓ Browser testing complete (Chrome, Firefox, Safari, Edge)
- ✓ Accessibility audit passed
- ✓ Security audit clean

### 7.2 Versioning
- **Semantic versioning**: Follow semver (MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH)
- **Release notes**: Document changes for each release
- **Breaking changes**: Clearly communicate breaking changes with migration guides

---

**This constitution is a living document. All team members are responsible for upholding these principles and suggesting improvements.**
